<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Food I Eat During a Celebration – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Food I Eat During a Celebration</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify numbers and quantities when heard slowly and clearly. Interactive: Follow directions in a game as instructed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify numbers and quantities when heard in festival contexts (e.g., "three cups of shaved ice"). Can follow directions to perform a simple game (e.g., "Toss the ball!"). Can recognize and repeat numbers related to festival items (e.g., "two balloons").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a short, upbeat festival music clip. The teacher shows large, colorful flashcards of festival foods (shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob) and other festival items (balloons, fireworks). The teacher points to each and asks, "What is this?" encouraging one-word answers or gestures. The teacher can say, "It's a party! I like music!" to set the scene.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "shaved ice" and clearly says, "Shaved ice! I like shaved ice." Then holds up "cotton candy" and says, "Cotton candy! It is fun!" The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /f/ in "fireworks" and /m/ in "meal" (using flashcards) and the initial sound /p/ in "party" by exaggerating the sounds and having students repeat chorally: "F-f-fireworks," "M-m-meal," "P-p-party." The teacher then counts items visually, "One balloon, two balloons," pointing and showing fingers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new vocabulary and numbers.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up a flashcard of cotton candy) "Is this shaved ice or cotton candy?" (Expected: "Cotton candy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up two fingers) "How many?" (Expected: "Two!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of colorful fireworks) "Are the fireworks bright?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording (or teacher's clear speaking) of short, simple sentences describing festival items and their quantities, accompanied by matching picture flashcards or a projected image. The content is an illustrative narrative featuring festival foods and items.
                <br>Example sentences:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is a festival! I see three balloons."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like cotton candy. I eat one cotton candy!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Look! Two shaved ices."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is a big party! I like music and big fireworks."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you like to eat? I like corn on the cob."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can identify numbers and quantities of festival items when heard slowly and clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the sentences and respond with actions (TPR) or by pointing to correct pictures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is the story about? Festivals or school?" (Expected: "Festivals!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> The teacher plays each sentence. Students listen and point to the flashcard that matches the spoken item and quantity. For example, "I see three balloons" -> students point to the picture of three balloons. "I eat one cotton candy" -> students point to one cotton candy.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying numbers (1-5) and specific festival foods and items. Grammar: "Subject + verb + adjective" (e.g., "It is fun!"), "Present simple" (e.g., "I like the meal."), and "WH-questions" (e.g., "What do you like?"). Vocabulary: festival, celebration, party, fireworks, meal, shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob, music, balloons. Pronunciation: /f/, /m/, /p/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listen and Point:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of festival items, some showing different quantities (e.g., one balloon, three balloons, two shaved ices, one corn on the cob). The teacher says sentences slowly and clearly, for example: "Point to three balloons!" "Point to one shaved ice!" "Point to two fireworks!" Students listen and point to the correct flashcard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice – Listen and Draw/Circle:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of festival items and numbers (1-5) next to them. The teacher calls out a sentence like, "Draw two cotton candies!" or "Circle four balloons!" Students listen to the number and the item and perform the action. The teacher ensures to pronounce /f/ and /m/ words clearly during this activity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Festival Says" Game:</b> In pairs, students sit opposite each other with a small set of flashcards. The teacher acts as "Festival Says" leader, giving commands like: "Festival says, 'Clap three times!'" "Festival says, 'Point to one corn on the cob!'" "Festival says, 'Toss the ball!'" (if they have a soft ball). Students listen and perform the actions. This practices following instructions and identifying numbers through TPR. The teacher reminds them to listen carefully for "Festival says."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Festival Season."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Festival Order Game." This is a listening-based game where students follow multi-step directions to create a simple "festival order."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students have blank paper and crayons. The teacher explains: "We are at a festival! I will tell you what to 'order' for your festival meal and decorations. Listen carefully!"
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a sequence of instructions using target vocabulary, numbers, and grammar. For example: "Draw two balloons!" (Pause for drawing). "Then, draw one big corn on the cob!" "Next, draw three shiny fireworks!" "I like festival music! Draw a happy face for music!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher walks around, observing students' drawings and listening skills.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After a few rounds, students can show their "festival order" drawings to a partner. The teacher models: "What do you like? I like the fireworks. They are bright." or "I like cotton candy!"</li>
                </ul>
                This activity encourages students to listen for specific items, quantities, and simple descriptive adjectives (big, shiny, happy). It also allows for simple pragmatic exchanges. </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Festival Order Game" and an individual "Listen and Check" exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>During the game:</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to note which students correctly draw the specified quantities and items from the listening instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Exit Ticket:</b> Students receive a short paper with 2-3 festival items (e.g., balloons, shaved ice) each with two different quantities (e.g., Picture A: 2 balloons, Picture B: 4 balloons). The teacher says, "Listen and circle the correct picture: 'Four balloons!'" or "Circle three shaved ices!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Listening:</b> Student correctly identifies numbers and quantities in instructions (e.g., draws two instead of three).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student can point to or draw the correct festival items (e.g., draws cotton candy when asked).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Directions:</b> Student successfully performs the actions specified in the game (e.g., circles the correct picture).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw their favorite festival food from today's lesson.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They then complete the sentence "I like (their drawing)!" or "It is (fun)!"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new festival food or item did you learn today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to hear the numbers? Show me with your thumb (up for easy, down for difficult)."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in introducing the vocabulary and numbers through listening activities? Which specific listening tasks were most engaging for 2nd graders? Were the pronunciation points clear? What adjustments are needed for students who struggled to identify numbers or follow multi-step directions? More TPR or visual aids might be beneficial, along with reviewing numbers 1-5 more slowly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will encounter these same festival food and item vocabulary words and numbers in short, illustrated sentences or a simple story to reinforce recognition and build foundational reading skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>